# Reconstruction of the Indium Tin Oxide Surface Enhances the Adsorption of High‐Density Self‐Assembled Monolayer for Perovskite/Silicon Tandem Solar Cells

## abstract
Self‐assembled monolayers (SAMs) are widely used as carrier transport interlayers for enabling high‐efficiency perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, achieving uniform and pinhole‐free monolayers on metal oxide (e.g., indium tin oxide, ITO) surfaces is still challenging due to the sensitivity of SAM adsorption to the complex oxide's surface chemistry. Here, the hydrofluoric acid and the subsequent UV–ozone treatment are employed to reconstruct the ITO surface by selectively removing the undesired terminal hydroxyl and hydrolysis product. This can significantly increase the ITO surface activity and area, thus facilitating the adsorption of high‐density SAMs. The resultant fluorinated surface can also prevent the direct contact of ITO with the perovskite active layer and passivate the perovskite bottom interface. Benefiting from the synergistically improved perovskite film formation, charge extraction, energy level alignment, and interfacial chemical stability, the corresponding PSC achieves a greatly enhanced power conversion efficiency of 21.3%, along with an enhanced long‐term stability as compared to the control counterpart. Furthermore, a semitransparent PSC with a certified efficiency of 19.0% (with a record fill factor of 84.1%) and a four‐terminal perovskite/silicon tandem with an efficiency of 28.4% are also demonstrated.

Self-assembled monolayers (SAMs) are widely used as carrier transport interlayers for enabling high-efficiency perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, achieving uniform and pinhole-free monolayers on metal oxide (e.g., indium tin oxide, ITO) surfaces is still challenging due to the sensitivity of SAM adsorption to the complex oxide’s surface chemistry. Here, the hydrofluoric acid and the subsequent UV–ozone treatment are employed to reconstruct the ITO surface by selectively removing the undesired terminal hydroxyl and hydrolysis product. This can significantly increase the ITO surface activity and area, thus facilitating the adsorption of high-density SAMs. The resultant fluorinated surface can also prevent the direct contact of ITO with the perovskite active layer and passivate the perovskite bottom interface. Benefiting from the synergistically improved perovskite film formation, charge extraction, energy level alignment, and interfacial chemical stability, the corresponding PSC achieves a greatly enhanced power conversion efficiency of $21.3\%$ , along with an enhanced long-term stability as compared to the control counterpart. Furthermore, a semitransparent PSC with a certified efficiency of $19.0\%$ (with a record fill factor of $84.1\%\$ ) and a four-terminal perovskite/silicon tandem with an efficiency of $28.4\%$ are also demonstrated.
## 1. Introduction
Self-assembled monolayers (SAMs) of small organic molecules have been extensively explored for perovskite based solar cells as carrier transport layers (CTLs), because of the advantages of molecular designability, cost-effectiveness, simple processing, excellent stability, absence of dopants, negligible parasitic absorption, conformal and loss-less electrical contacts, and large-area scalability.[1–6] These materials can be chemically grafted to various metal and metal oxide surfaces to form monolayers via anchoring groups such as carboxylic acid $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{I-C}}=\mathrm{O})\mathrm{OH}$ , CA], phosphonic acid $\scriptstyle[-\mathrm{P}(=\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , PA], sulfonic acid $\scriptstyle[-\mathrm{{S}}(=\mathrm{{O}})(\mathrm{{OH}})_{2}$ , SA], silyl trialkoxide $[-\mathrm{Si}(\mathrm{OR})_{3}$ , STO], boronic acid [─B(OH)2, BA], thiols, and alkenes.[6–10] Among them, PA-based SAMs (PA-SAMs) have attracted increased interest in recent years for their thermal stabilities, hydrolysis resistances, high surface coverages as well as lack of homocondensation.[8] By properly optimizing the molecules sizes, engineering the position of substituents, and selecting the specific functional groups of PA-SAMs,[11,12] the perovskite crystallization, defect passivation, and interface carrier selectivity can be synergistically improved, thus significantly promoting both the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) and stabilities of the perovskite based solar cells. Of note, they have also dominated recent record and high PCEs for monolithic perovskite/silicon, perovskite/CIGS, perovskite/organic, and all-perovskite tandem solar cells,[13–17] further demonstrating the effectiveness and superiority of the PA-SAMs.
Generally, the linkage of PA coupling molecules to metal oxide surfaces involves the first coordination of the phosphoryl oxygen to a Lewis acidic site on the surface, followed by the heterocondensation with surface hydroxyl, and the hydrogen bonding of surface hydroxyl and residual P─OH and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ groups.[11] Therefore, the surface chemistry of the metal oxide, especially the hydroxyl concentration and reactivity, plays a crucial role for tuning the adsorption mode, density, ordering, thickness, surface coverage, and molecular conformation of PA-SAMs.[18] However, there are two types of hydroxyl groups or ions that differ in acidity on the surface of metal oxides: the terminal hydroxyl $(\mathrm{OH_{T}})$ bonding to a single metal atom site and the bridging hydroxyl $(\mathrm{OH_{B}})$ spanning two or more metal atom sites. The former would expect a tendency for dissociation as $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions and thus should be predominantly basic in character, while the latter should be strongly polarized by the cations, thus loosening the bond to hydrogen and resulting in acidic character.[19] This amphoteric character of hydroxyl groups can inevitably disturb the adsorption of the PA and in turn the achievable packing density of SAMs. Additionally, different from the doubly-bonded $\mathrm{{OH_{B}}}$ , the electric charges of the singly-bonded $\mathrm{{OH_{T}}}$ are not neutralized symmetrically by lattice cations due to the local imbalance of anion and cation arrangements at the surface,[20] resulting in a strong electrostatic repulsion between the $\mathrm{{OH}_{T}}$ and then leading to a looser packing of the adsorbed PA-SAMs. Moreover, it is well known that the commercially available indium tin oxide (ITO) substrates show high concentrations of hydroxides and oxy-hydroxides, mainly ${\mathrm{In}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and InOOH (the hydrolysis constant of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is a factor of $10^{10}$ larger than that of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ) induced during the cleaning processes, in the near-surface region.[21] The SAMs adsorbed on these poorly conductive “dead regions” are typically disconnected from the conductive ITO lattice, that is, only binding sites on the surface of stoichiometric oxides (“hot spots”) are truly electroactive for the adsorption, thus slowing the charge extraction.[22] Meanwhile, since these hydrolysis products are not securely attached to the ITO lattice,[23] the hydroxide together with the adsorbed SAMs can be easily removed from the ITO surface during the subsequent washing procedure. This can result in the direct contact of the perovskite to the ITO substrate and then induce a large leakage current at the interface, ultimately undermining the device performance and stability.
Despite the importance of such interface chemistry in the SAMs adsorption, only little attention has been reported on increasing the packing density of SAM-based CTL in perovskite solar cells (PSCs). For instance, the anchor strength and packing density of SAMs on ITO surface can be promoted by adopting a co-assembled monolayer strategy, in which small-sized molecules are mixed into large-sized PA-SAMs solutions as fillers to passivate the exposed ITO surface and increase the coverage of the formed monolayers.[6,15,24,25] Alternatively, a thermal pretreatment of ITO at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ has also been demonstrated to effectively modulate the amounts of OH groups and oxygen vacancies on the ITO surface for achieving uniform SAMs.[26] Recently, our previous work has verified that the introduction of a OH group-rich $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ seed layer on ITO surface can significantly increase the density of PA-SAMs by switching the PA binding mode from bidentate to tridentate.[18] This strategy is also confirmed by other groups and further extended to all-perovskite and perovskite/silicon tandem applications.[15,27,28] However, directly tailoring the chemical composition of the ITO surface to enhance its interaction, on the molecular level, with SAM materials is not explored yet for the perovskite community, and a detailed exploration of the adsorption kinetics of SAMs modulated by the ITO surface is still lacking.
Here, we present a simple and effective method for reconstructing the ITO surface to increase the density of adsorbed SAMs, thereby enhancing the efficiency and stability of the SAMbased PSCs. It is found that the undesired terminal hydroxyls and hydrolysis products at the ITO surface can be selectively removed by the hydrofluoric acid (HF) and subsequent UV–ozone (UVO) treatment, which can increase the ITO surface activity and area, thus facilitating the SAM adsorption. Meanwhile, the resultant fluorinated surfaces can not only prevent the direct contact of ITO with the perovskite active layer, but also effectively passivate the perovskite buried defects by forming strong hydrogen and ionic bonds with perovskite lattices. This strategy can ameliorate the perovskite film formation, and simultaneously improve the charge extraction, energy level alignment, and chemical stability of the hole-selective interface. As a result, the corresponding PSC achieves a greatly enhanced PCE of $21.3\%$ , along with an enhanced long-term stability as compared to its control counterpart. Finally, we fabricate a semitransparent PSC with a certified PCE of $19.0\%$ (with a record FF of $84.1\%$ , and demonstrate a $28.4\%$ perovskite/silicon four-terminal tandem efficiency when mechanically stacked on a tunnel oxide passivating contact (TOPCon) silicon bottom cell, which is the highest reported for perovskite/TOPCon four-terminal tandem solar cells (Main Text Paragraph).
## 2. Results and Discussion
