To gain a more comprehensive understanding of the difference in the ITO surface termination induced by the surface reconstruction, complementary Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) measurements were performed at each step of the surface modification. Figure S5, Supporting Information, shows the infrared reflection-absorption spectra of the ITO surface after the UVO cleaning step and subsequent HF treatment (green line) and after an additional UV treatment (yellow line). Both spectra were acquired in situ immediately and were related to a background measured prior to the respective treatment. For the pristine HF-treated ITO, a broad and asymmetrical absorption band assigned to the hydrogen bonding can be observed in the range of $3100{-}3500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ with a maximum at $3255~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ,[34,35] suggesting the increased hydrogen bonding in the oxide layers induced by the HF treatment. After an additional UVO treatment, the differential infrared absorption spectrum shows a slightly increase of the hydrogen bonding. This can be explained with a partial transformation of $-\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{T}}\mathbf{s}$ to $-\mathrm{OH_{B}s}$ during the UVO treatment, in good agreement with the XPS results. The FTIR measurements were also conducted at the end of the MeO-2PACz adsorption process, referenced to the respective surfaces before adsorption, to demonstrate the enhanced adsorption of MeO-2PACz on the HF-treated ITO surface. Figure 1d presents the spectra for molecules of MeO-2PACz, comparing monolayers on ITO surfaces with and without the surface reconstruction, versus the bulk spectrum obtained from the powder pressed into a KBr pellet. The spectral features change significantly upon treating the ITO surface with MeO-2PACz, substantiating the successful adsorption of MeO-2PACz. The absorption peaks of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ $1160{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ ) and $_{\mathrm{P-O-H}}$ (1025 and $947\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) can be used to probe the interaction between MeO-2PACz and oxides. For both samples, the presence of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{P=O}}$ together with the loss of the $(\mathrm{P-O-H})$ mode may indicate the absence of the purely tridentate (through three $_{\mathrm{P-O-M}}$ bonds, no free polar moieties) and monodentate (through a $_{\mathrm{P-O-In}}$ bond, with free $_{\mathrm{P-O-H}}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ moieties) bonding configurations previously suggested. The appearance of the additional P─O─In mode at $1036~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ further provides strong evidence for the predominantly bidentate (through two P─O─In bonds, with free $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{P=O}}$ moiety) attachment of MeO-2PACz to the ITO surface, in line with the previous reports.[18] Additionally, compared with the control surface without surface reconstruction, the reconstructed surface exhibits a slightly decreased intensity of the remaining free P─O─H mode at $947~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 1d) and the hydrogen bonding mode at $3255~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information), indicating that more P─O─H can be converted to P─O─In bonds and thus confirming the enhanced density of the adsorbed MeO-2PACz on the reconstructed surface. This adsorption enhancement can be further verified by the atomic force microscopy–based infrared spectroscopy (AFM-IR) (Figure S6, Supporting Information), where the reconstructed sample exhibits a higher IR response.
We then investigated the effects of the surface reconstruction on the surface morphology, structure, electrical conductivity, and optical absorption of the ITO substrate. The pristine ITO surface shows a typical grain–subgrain polycrystalline structure (Figure 2a). Each grain ( $50{-}200\mathrm{nm}$ in size) consists of a cluster of $10{-}30\mathrm{nm}$ sized subgrains, which are highly aligned in crystalline orientation.[36] After the surface reconstruction, the larger grain structure of the ITO surface can be clearly resolved, but the subgrain structure is less clear (Figure 2b), indicating the preferential dissolution of grain boundaries, that is, fusion of the subgrains. This preferential grain boundary dissolution can be explained by that the grain boundaries offer preferential dissolution sites for releasing the metallic ions ( $\mathrm{[n^{3+}}$ and $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ ) and oxygen ion ${{0}^{2-}}$ due to their own high diffusion rate path and stored energy.[37] It is well known that the grain boundary is rich in the undercoordinated metal sites which are therefore expected to be quite sensitive with the atmosphere and residual water to produce an inactive surface with extensive hydroxylation and carbonate-like reaction products, hindering the effective adsorption of MeO2PACz.[23] Therefore, the fusion of the subgrains induced by surface reconstruction can significantly decrease these inactive areas, thus increasing the active site density at the ITO surface for the MeO-2PACz adsorption.[38] Concomitantly, the surface root mean square is increased from 3.6 to $7.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ with the surface area (SA) enlarged from 1.03 to $1.05~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , as observed in the AFM images (Figure S7, Supporting Information). As will be discussed below, this enlarged surface can assist the carrier separation and extraction by providing larger interfacial area between perovskite and adjacent contacts. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S8, Supporting Information) of both the pristine and reconstructed samples show four distinct diffraction signals located at $21.3^{\circ}$ , $30.4^{\circ}$ , $35.3^{\circ}$ , and $37.6^{\circ}$ for (211), (222), (400), and (411) crystal planes of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , respectively, confirming the polycrystalline nature of the ITO. Notably, no significant differences can be found in the intensity and the full-width at half-maximum of the diffraction peaks, suggesting that the surface reconstruction does not impact the crystallinity and the crystal structure of the ITO films. Additionally, the reconstruction does not noticeably change the electronic properties (e.g., the electrical sheet resistance and Hall mobility) (Figure S9, Supporting Information), the optical absorption (Figure S10, Supporting Information), and the surface energy (Figure S11, Supporting Information) of the ITO films. These results indicate that the surface reconstruction can effectively increase the ITO surface activity and area but without altering its bulk film properties.
It is well known that the perovskite film formation is susceptible to the surface property of the substrate, we investigated the influence of the ITO reconstruction on the quality and surface morphology of the top-deposited perovskite film, wide-bandgap perovskite films $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}(\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathbf{MA}_{0.17})_{0.95}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathbf{Br}_{0.17})_{3}$ , prepared via a simple one-step anti-solvent recipe with the structures of pristine $\mathrm{ITO/MeO-}2\mathrm{PACz}_{I}$ perovskite (denoted as the control) and reconstructed ITO/MeO-2PACz/perovskite (denoted as the target). The UV–vis absorption spectra exhibit similar absorption onset for both samples with an identical optical bandgap of $1.63\mathrm{eV}$ extracted from Tauc plots (Figure S12, Supporting Information), suggesting that the ITO reconstruction has little impact on the perovskite absorption intensity and absorption edge. As shown from the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure 2c,d), the average grain size of the target perovskite $(277\mathrm{nm})$ is much larger than that of the control one $(197\mathrm{nm})$ ), which benefits a reduction in the grain boundary area for the enhanced perovskite film quality. The improved perovskite crystallinity can be further confirmed by the XRD measurements (Figure S13, Supporting Information). The crystal structure is the same for both perovskite films, but the diffraction intensity of the target perovskite is stronger than that of the control film, suggesting a higher crystallinity and larger average crystal size.[39] We attribute this improved perovskite film quality to the bridging effect of the fluorinated surface, that is, the presence of $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{T}}\mathbf{s}$ . As reported, during the perovskite film formation, the fluoride ions with the highest electronegativity can staunchly immobilize both the organic cations and halide anions and retard the perovskite grain growth through the strong hydrogen bonding and ionic bonding, contributing to the double effect of crystal growth regulation and defect passivation.[40,41] Therefore, we then employed dark space-charge-limited current (SCLC) measurements to determine the density of defects by tracking the trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ threshold of current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves for hole-only devices (Figure 2e). The target device shows a lower hole trap density $(7.1\times10^{14}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ than the control one $(1.2\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}. $ ), which demonstrate that the defect density is greatly reduced in the target perovskite film due to the increased perovskite grain sizes as well as the enhanced passivation effect of the high-density MeO-2PACz and $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{T}}\mathbf{s}$ . We also examined the leakage current from the dark $J{-}V$ curves of the full perovskite devices (Figure 2f). The reverse leakage current and the ideal factor (n) of the target device is smaller than the control one, suggesting the suppressed non-radiative recombination, in agreement with the increased recombination resistance fitted in the impedance spectroscopy (Figures S14 and S15, Supporting Information), the decreased low frequency capacitance indicated by the capacitance-frequency measurements (Figure S16, Supporting Information).
Since the carrier selectivity of the MeO-2PACz monolayer is very sensitive to its adsorption density, we next examined the impact of the surface reconstruction on the carrier transport dynamic at the perovskite/MeO-2PACz interface. The steady-state photoluminescence (SSPL) spectra show a higher quenching efficiency of $89.0\%$ for the reconstructed sample, while the pristine sample has a quenching efficiency of $73.4\%$ (Figure 3a), indicating that the increased density of MeO-2PACz on the reconstructed ITO surface is beneficial for the efficient hole extraction from the perovskite film. We also used time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy to quantitatively estimate the dynamics of charge carriers (Figure 3b and Table S3, Supporting Information). The lifetimes of the reconstructed sample were calculated as having a fast decay component $\left\langle\tau_{1}\right\rangle$ , attributed to the quenching of charge carries by the CTLs) of 42.2 ns and a slow decay component $(\tau_{2}$ , attributed to the radiative recombination of free carriers in bulk films) of 162.4 ns, while the control sample showed a larger $\tau_{1}$ of 91.8 ns and a comparable $\tau_{2}$ of 159.4 ns, confirming that the high-density MeO2PACz leads to dynamically faster hole extraction. The scanning Kelvin probe microscopy measurements were then performed to estimate the effect of surface reconstruction on the surface electronic properties of the ITO as well as the subsequently adsorbed MeO-2PACz (Figure 3c and Figure S17, Supporting Information). The average surface contact potential difference (CPD) of the reconstructed ITO $(-131.6\mathrm{mV})$ is much lower than that of the pristine one $(-110.7\mathrm{mV})$ , indicating the increased work function (WF) of ITO induced by the surface reconstruction. Here, the large electronegativity difference between metal and halogen atoms (i.e., 1.78 for In compared to 3.98 for F in $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{T}}$ on the reconstructed surface) can introduce a strong negative surface dipole layer to upward shift the vacuum energy level, thus resulting in the increased WF.[42] After the MeO-2PACz adsorption, the CPD of the pristine and reconstructed ITO is further decreased to −145.9 and $-196.8\mathrm{mV},$ respectively. The larger CPD shift of the reconstructed ITO as compared with the pristine one is a good indicator of the increased density of the adsorbed MeO-2PACz, that is, the promoted MeO-2PACz adsorption on the reconstructed ITO surface.[32] This can be further supported by the ultra-violet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements (Figure 3d), where the WF is increased from $4.54\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine ITO/MeO-2PACz to $4.57~\mathrm{eV}$ for the reconstructed
ITO/MeO-2PACz. The single-carrier devices with the structure of ITO (pristine or reconstructed)/MeO-2PACz/MoOx/Ag were further fabricated to demonstrate the effect of the surface reconstruction on the hole transport ability of the adsorbed MeO$2\mathrm{PACz}$ (Figure 3e).[39] Compared with the control device based on the pristine ITO, the device based on the reconstructed ITO exhibits a strong ohmic behavior with a decreased hole rectification ratio $(K_{\mathrm{hole}}=\lvert J(-0.5\mathrm{\:V})/J(+0.5\mathrm{\:V})\rvert)$ from 1.02 to 1.00 and an increased slope from 0.46 to 0.57, indicating the improved hole selectivity of MeO-2PACz on the reconstructed ITO surface.[18] After the perovskite deposition, the CPD of the perovskite surface is increased from $-623.1\mathrm{mV}$ for the control film to $-608.6\mathrm{mV}$ for the target perovskite based on the reconstructed ITO (Figure 3f and Figure S18, Supporting Information). This positive CPD shift evidences the n-type doping of the perovskite, thus facilitating the efficient electron extraction from perovskite to electron transport layer.[39] Therefore, such the enhanced built-in electric fields at both hole- and electron- selective contacts can significantly strengthen the driving force for the photogenerated carriers separation across the whole device, in good accordance with the Mott–Schottky measurements (Figure S19, Supporting Information).
To further examine the effects of the ITO reconstruction at the device level, we fabricated PSCs with a reconstructed
ITO/MeO-2PACz/Perovskite/ $\mathrm{\DeltaC}_{60}$ /bathocuproine $(\mathrm{BCP})/\mathrm{Ag}$ structure (Figure 4a). The control device with the traditional ITO without surface reconstruction was also used for comparison. The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics for best-performing devices with and without ITO reconstruction under a simulated AM1.5G $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) sunlight are shown in Figure 4b, and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 1. The control devices realized a PCE of $19.5\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.10{\mathrm{V}},$ a $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ of $21.8~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and a FF of $81.4\%$ . In contrast, the target device based on the reconstructed ITO achieved an enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.13\ \mathrm{V},$ an increased $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.7\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a boosted FF of $83.1\%$ , contributing to a high PCE of $21.3\%$ . The stabilized power output measurements were also performed by holding the devices at a fixed maximum power point (MPP) voltage to accurately access the device performance, achieving a stabilized efficiency of $19.4\%$ and $20.6\%$ for the control and target device, respectively, under the continuous measurement in the first $600\mathrm{~s~}$ (Figure 4c). The increase of $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ can be verified by the external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements (Figure 4d). The target device exhibits a considerably improved EQE response in the whole wavelength range as compared to the control device, consistent with the improved charge extraction at the perovskite/hole transport layer interface. Integration of EQE spectra with an
AM1.5G reference spectrum yields photocurrents of 20.8 and $21.4\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the control and target devices, respectively, in agreement with the $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ values measured from $J{-}V$ curves. The internal quantum efficiency (IQE) measurements were further carried out by using $\mathrm{EQE}=(1-\mathrm{reflectance})\times\mathrm{IQE}$ to evaluate the carrier collection efficiency in the devices. Compared with the control device, there is an enhanced IQE response in the whole wavelength range for the target device (Figure 4e). The extracted EQE and IQE improvement ratios are similar values at 1.03 and 1.02, respectively, confirming that the EQE enhancement induced by the ITO reconstruction comes mostly from the improvement in the carrier extraction rather than the light absorption.[39] The operational stability of fabricated devices was monitored by maximum power point tracking under continuous illumination system equipped with a xenon lamp under an ambient environment with $\approx60\%$ relative humidity (RH) without temperature control and encapsulation. The PCE of the control device decreased to $77.8\%$ of the initial value after $107\mathrm{h}$ of illumination, while the target device still retained $86.0\%$ of the initial PCE after $660\mathrm{h}$ . These considerable improvements in the efficiency and stability of the target devices can be attributed to the stabilized local structure at the fluoride bridged interface, the suppressed defect-mediated non-radiative recombination in the perovskite film, and the improved carrier extraction at interfacial contacts. Moreover, the generality of this reconstruction strategy can also be validated by using different ITO substrates (Figure S20, Supporting Information), SAM molecular systems (Figures S21 and S22, Supporting Information), and deposition methods (Figure S23, Supporting Information).
Finally, we utilized this reconstructed ITO in four-terminal mechanically stacked perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells (Figure 5a). Semitransparent PSCs were fabricated using the configuration of glass/reconstructed ITO/MeO2PACz/perovskite/ $\mathrm{^{\prime}C_{60}}/$ BCP:Ag/indium zinc oxide (IZO)/MgFx, in which the multifunctional BCP:Ag complex was employed as the buffer layer to prevent the sputtering damage during the deposition of IZO transparent electrode[43] and the MgFx was used as the antireflection coating. The performance of each sub-cell is presented in Figure 5b and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 2. The semitransparent PSC shows a champion PCE of $19.2\%$ with an outstanding FF exceeding $83\%$ (certified PCE of $19.0\%$ with a record FF of $84.1\%$ , Figure S24, Supporting Information), which is the highest FF among those of the reported semitransparent PSCs. Under the semitransparent PSC filter, the bottom silicon solar cell, TOPCon silicon cell with an original PCE of $24.3\%$ , retains the PCE of $9.2\%$ . The reduced PCE of the filtered silicon solar cell is mainly ascribed to the sharp decline in $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ from 40.2 to $17.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which is in accordance with the corresponding EQE spectra (Figure 5c). This can be attributed to the absorption of most of the short wavelength photons $(300-760\ \mathrm{nm})$ by the top semitransparent PSC, thus only allowing the absorption of long wavelength photons by the bottom silicon solar cell. Additionally, the light intensity reaching the filtered silicon bottom cell is lower than that of the unfiltered device, thus leading to a slightly lower $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Overall, the PCE of the fourterminal perovskite/silicon tandem solar cell is calculated to be $28.4\%$ , which is the highest reported for perovskite/TOPCon four-terminal tandem solar cells, confirming the effectiveness of the ITO surface reconstruction strategy.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, the ITO surface is reconstructed by the HF and subsequent UVO treatment to selectively remove the undesired terminal hydroxyls and hydrolysis products from the ITO surface, leading to increased surface activity and area without affecting the bulk film properties. This, in turn, facilitates the adsorption of high-density SAMs onto the reconstructed ITO surface. Additionally, the fluorinated surface can also prevent the direct contact of ITO with the perovskite active layer and passivate perovskite defects. Benefited from the synergistically improved perovskite film formation, charge extraction, energy level alignment, and interfacial chemical stability, the corresponding PSC achieves a greatly enhanced PCE of $21.3\%$ (with a stabilized PCE of $20.6\%$ ), as well as an enhanced long-term stability compared to its control counterpart. Furthermore, we fabricate a semitransparent PSC with a certified PCE of $19.0\%$ with a record FF of $84.1\%$ and demonstrate a $28.4\%$ perovskite/silicon four-terminal tandem efficiency when mechanically stacked on a TOPCon silicon bottom cell.
